 CODE
2.3.19. 
Personal property 
The following are personal property, irrespective of the time of acquisition 
1¡ clothing and objects for personal use; 
2¡ literary, artistic or industrial property rights; 
3¡ the right to pensions, life annuities or allowances of the same kind, of which only one of the spouses is the owner; 
4¡ the right to compensation for personal bodily or moral injury;
5¡ the rights resulting from the status of partner attached to company shares acquired with joint funds and which have been registered in the name of one of the spouses, including the right to act as the owner of such shares, provided that the company is subject to legal or statutory rules, or to agreements between shareholders, which restrict the transfer of shares, or is a company in which only that spouse exercises his or her professional activity as manager or director; 
6¡ the right to property which a spouse uses exclusively for the exercise of his profession or the operation of his business, including the right to act as owner of such professional property, unless the spouses exercise this profession together or operate this business together; 
7¡ the right to the clientele, including the right to act as owner of the clientele, unless the clientele has been constituted or acquired in the context of a profession which the spouses exercise together or of a business which they operate together.
Are also personal property:
1¡ compensation paid to a spouse as reparation for damage, insofar as such compensation is intended to make good his or her personal incapacity, which concerns the non-economically quantifiable consequences of the impairment of his or her physical and psychological integrity in his or her daily life; 
2¡ the insurance benefit related to an individual life insurance contract which was entered into by one of the spouses during the regime, and which is due for the benefit of the other spouse at the dissolution of the regime.
